@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `usca-operation-of-statutes`
IMPORT `usca-canons-of-reading`

-- Tests of usca-canons-of-reading.l4.  Each expected value is decided from the section cited above the assertion, before the run.
-- A failing assertion is a finding.

§ `Gov. Code § 6: headings do not affect the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the code`

#ASSERT NOT (`the element may affect the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the Government Code` `a title heading`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the element may affect the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the Government Code` `a division heading`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the element may affect the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the Government Code` `a part heading`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the element may affect the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the Government Code` `a chapter heading`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the element may affect the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the Government Code` `an article heading`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the element may affect the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the Government Code` `a section heading`)
-- Outside the encoding: the text says nothing about the operative text.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the element may affect the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the Government Code` `the operative text of a provision`
-- Added after the independent test pass: the section names six kinds of heading; a subdivision's or a paragraph's is not among them.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the element may affect the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the Government Code` `a subdivision heading` BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 6 names title, division, part, chapter, article and section headings; it does not say whether the heading of a subdivision affects the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the code"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the element may affect the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the Government Code` `a paragraph heading` BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 6 names title, division, part, chapter, article and section headings; it does not say whether the heading of a paragraph affects the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the code"

§ `Gov. Code § 10, Civ. Code § 14(b)(6), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b)(9): section and subdivision`

#ASSERT `the section that the word section refers to when` FALSE EQUALS `a section of this code`
#ASSERT `the section that the word section refers to when` TRUE EQUALS `a section of the other code or statute`
#ASSERT `the subdivision that the word subdivision refers to when` FALSE EQUALS `a subdivision of the section in which the term occurs`
#ASSERT `the subdivision that the word subdivision refers to when` TRUE EQUALS `a subdivision of the section expressly mentioned`

§ `Gov. Code § 11, Civ. Code § 14(a), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(a): tense`

-- Gov. Code § 11: the present tense includes the past and future tenses; and the future, the present.
#ASSERT `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Government Code` `the present tense` `the past tense`
#ASSERT `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Government Code` `the present tense` `the future tense`
#ASSERT `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Government Code` `the future tense` `the present tense`
#ASSERT `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Government Code` `the present tense` `the present tense`
#ASSERT `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Government Code` `the past tense` `the past tense`
#ASSERT `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Government Code` `the future tense` `the future tense`
-- Not said by Gov. Code § 11: the future including the past, the past including anything else.
#ASSERT REFUSED `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Government Code` `the future tense` `the past tense` BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 11 says the present tense includes the past and future tenses and the future the present; it does not say whether the future tense includes the past"
#ASSERT REFUSED `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Government Code` `the past tense` `the present tense` BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 11 says the present tense includes the past and future tenses and the future the present; it does not say what the past tense includes"
#ASSERT REFUSED `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Government Code` `the past tense` `the future tense`
-- Civ. Code § 14(a) and Code Civ. Proc. § 17(a): the present tense includes the future as well as the present; nothing of the past (fork F-tense).
#ASSERT `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Civil Code` `the present tense` `the future tense`
#ASSERT `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Code of Civil Procedure` `the present tense` `the future tense`
#ASSERT `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Civil Code` `the present tense` `the present tense`
#ASSERT REFUSED `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Civil Code` `the present tense` `the past tense` BECAUSE "Civ. Code § 14(a) and Code Civ. Proc. § 17(a) say words in the present tense include the future as well as the present; they say nothing of the past tense or of the future tense including the present"
#ASSERT REFUSED `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Code of Civil Procedure` `the present tense` `the past tense`
#ASSERT REFUSED `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Civil Code` `the future tense` `the present tense`
#ASSERT REFUSED `a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` `the Code of Civil Procedure` `the past tense` `the present tense`

§ `Gov. Code § 12, Civ. Code § 14(a), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(a): gender`

#ASSERT `a word used in the gender includes the gender, in` `the Government Code` `the masculine gender` `the feminine gender`
#ASSERT `a word used in the gender includes the gender, in` `the Government Code` `the masculine gender` `the neuter gender`
#ASSERT `a word used in the gender includes the gender, in` `the Civil Code` `the masculine gender` `the feminine gender`
#ASSERT `a word used in the gender includes the gender, in` `the Civil Code` `the masculine gender` `the neuter gender`
#ASSERT `a word used in the gender includes the gender, in` `the Code of Civil Procedure` `the masculine gender` `the feminine gender`
#ASSERT `a word used in the gender includes the gender, in` `the Code of Civil Procedure` `the masculine gender` `the neuter gender`
#ASSERT `a word used in the gender includes the gender, in` `the Government Code` `the feminine gender` `the feminine gender`
-- Outside the encoding: the feminine or the neuter including another gender.
#ASSERT REFUSED `a word used in the gender includes the gender, in` `the Government Code` `the feminine gender` `the masculine gender`
#ASSERT REFUSED `a word used in the gender includes the gender, in` `the Civil Code` `the neuter gender` `the feminine gender`
#ASSERT REFUSED `a word used in the gender includes the gender, in` `the Code of Civil Procedure` `the feminine gender` `the neuter gender`

§ `Gov. Code § 13, Civ. Code § 14(a), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(a): number`

#ASSERT `a word used in the number includes the number, in` `the Government Code` `the singular number` `the plural number`
